+The comparison that finds a row is textual: it reads the value each side writes, so a row can
+record a difference in notation whose computed value matches, such as `transparent` against
+`rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)` or `0.875em` against `87.5%`. The member each row takes comes from the value
+each side resolves to in Chromium instead, which the `ValueResolver` class reads. It resolves the
+release's value against the release's stylesheet and Veneer's value against the built cascade, each
+on elements built from the row's selector, so every variable a value reads takes the value its own
+stylesheet gives it there. It then computes both on twin elements of one neutral page, in the row's
+mode and at the default factors, so a relative length reads the same context on both sides. Where
+the release writes the property more than once at the row's site, the release value is the last
+declaration Chromium parses, as it is for the `-webkit-sticky` fallback the release writes ahead of
+`sticky`. A custom property computes through the first typed probe both of its values parse: a
+registered syntax such as `<length>` or `<color>`, and after every syntax, a standard property such
+as `box-shadow` or `font-family`. The gate names every row the resolver cannot decide and expects
+none, so no row takes the member its text alone would name.
 
 A `—` cell in the `Condition` column records a declaration under no at-rule, and a `—` cell in
 either value column records a side that writes no declaration there at all. An `(empty)` cell
